we're joined now by the head of personal  finance at AJ Bell Laura Suter Laura  hello to you let's go through some of  the findings that have come out today  there's so much new information about  the uh economy out there at the moment  let's drill into these um unemployment  figures first the new data showing that  the UK's unemployment figures had fallen  to its lowest rate in decades can you  put this into context and explain the  significance and importance of this yeah  of course so if we look at this is the  unemployment rate going all the way back  to the 1970s on what it shows at this  end which is the current day is that  it's dropped significantly so we're at a  lowest rate for almost 50 years it's 3.5  which usually is a very good thing  that's usually a positive thing however  what we're seeing at the moment if we go  on to this next slide is this red line  here shows the number of vacancies so  the number of companies trying to hire  at the moment with job vacancies and the  blue line is the number of unemployed  people in the UK now if we look very at  the end here they've crossed over which  effectively means there's more vacancies  than there are people to fill them and  that obviously presents an issue for  companies but also for the economy yeah  so that looks like that's going to be an  ongoing issue can we talk about  um people that are economically inactive  and why that is what's the story behind  the stats yeah so it's a it's not a very  catchy phrase but um effectively there's  three groups of people people that are  in employment so that have jobs people  that are unemployed but they're looking  for work and then the economically  inactive which are people that are out  of work but they're not looking to get  into work and that could be for a  variety of reasons and what we see here  so um over nearer you is that economic  inactivity rate that you can see is  ticked up a bit towards the end and here  we have the age breakdown of the people  